const title = "Samsung Dryer Not Heating? Causes & Fix — Same Day";
const description = "Samsung dryer runs but won't heat, or shows HE/hE? Usually a clogged vent (overheat) — a fire risk worth acting on same-day — then the heating element or thermistor. What to check and when it's a tech job.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Samsung dryer not heating?",
    a: "On a Samsung dryer that tumbles but won't heat, the most common cause isn't a broken part at all — it's a restricted exhaust vent. A clogged vent traps heat, the dryer overheats, and Samsung's safety system either trips a thermal fuse or throws an HE/hE overheat code and cuts the heat to protect itself. After the vent, the usual failed parts are the heating element and the thermistor (temperature sensor). On a gas Samsung dryer, a weak igniter, a failed gas-valve coil, or a bad flame sensor stops the burner from lighting. Clean the vent first; if it still won't heat, it's a diagnostic."
  },
  {
    q: "What does the HE or hE code mean on a Samsung dryer?",
    a: "HE (or hE) is a heating error — the dryer detected an overheat and shut the heat off to protect itself. The overwhelming majority of the time the cause is a clogged lint filter or exhaust vent choking the airflow, which makes the dryer run too hot. Clean the filter every load and clear the full exhaust vent, then run it again. If HE returns on a genuinely clear vent, the thermistor is misreading the temperature or the heating system has a fault, and that's a tech job. HE is a safety feature doing its job, not a random glitch."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I fix a Samsung dryer that won't heat myself?",
    a: "The safe and most effective step is yours to do: clean the lint filter and clear the entire exhaust vent, end to end, including the flexible hose behind the dryer and the exterior flap. That resolves a large share of no-heat and HE-code calls and, just as important, removes a genuine fire hazard. Also check your breaker — an electric dryer runs on a 240V double breaker, and if only one leg trips the drum still tumbles but the heat is dead, so reset both. What you should not do is open the dryer cabinet to test the heating element or thermal fuse, and never disturb the gas line or gas valve on a gas dryer. Those are tech jobs."
  },
  {
    q: "Is a dryer that won't heat a fire risk?",
    a: "The thing that most often causes a no-heat Samsung dryer — a lint-clogged vent — is also a leading cause of house fires. The U.S. Fire Administration counts roughly 15,600 dryer fires a year, and the NFPA finds that failure to clean the dryer and its venting is the leading factor, with lint the most common material to ignite. That's why a dryer running hot or throwing an HE overheat code is worth acting on the same day rather than living with it. Clearing the vent fixes the heat and removes the hazard at once."
  },
  {
    q: "Is it worth repairing a Samsung dryer that won't heat?",
    a: "Usually yes — the no-heat parts are among the more affordable dryer repairs. A heating element, thermistor, or thermal fuse on an electric Samsung, or an igniter, gas-valve coil, or flame sensor on a gas model, is a modest fix against a dryer you're keeping. Our techs verify the vent first (there's no point replacing a fuse that blew because of a blocked vent — it'll just blow again), then test the heating circuit and replace the failed part. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair."
  }
];

